1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Hi, I'm Robin Bridges and I'm an assistant scoutmaster with Troop 466 in Huntsville, Alabama,

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:07,000
the Rocket City.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,000
This addition is a scoutmaster podcast that's sponsored by backers like me.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Hey, thanks a lot, Clark, for what you did.

5
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,000
And now to you, scoutmaster,

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,000
And you say your first name, Darnell.

7
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Yeah, can you imagine? that made me a pretty good freighter about the third grade.

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Listen, Clark was no prize.

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Yeah, I can imagine You and I are probably both pretty good fighters.

10
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Hey, everybody, this is podcast number 222..

11
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Hey,

12
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Well, hello and welcome back to the Scoutmaster podcast.

13
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,000
This is Clark Green

14
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,000
And this week, oh my, you're not going to hear all that much that I record this week.

15
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:19,000
As you can tell, I'm working on a pretty good spring cold here.

16
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:22,000
I'm going to put off reading messages out of the mail bag this week.

17
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:28,000
I just want to take a very few moments at the top of the podcast to thank Jim Appelly

18
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:34,000
and Robin Bridges who have become ScoutmasterCGcom backers over the past week,

19
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:40,000
And backers are people who step up and help make the podcast and all the other resources we create possible.

20
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
If you're interested in becoming a backer, check out ScoutmasterCGcom

21
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,000
and go to the support link at the top of the page

22
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:50,000
And you'll get details about how to do that.

23
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:56,000
I also wanted to make sure to tell you this week that I put together a package of PDF documents-

24
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:02,000
over 40 infographics and other documents that you may find useful- in PDF format

25
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,000
that you can print out and use in your program.

26
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:11,000
I put these together in a downloadable zip file to make it easy to get those resources

27
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,000
And once again, go to ScoutmasterCGcom.

28
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Look for the link to the ScoutmasterCG PDF package at the top of the page

29
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,000
And you can find out how to get that downloadable file.

30
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Well, this week on the podcast- thank goodness,

31
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:31,000
I recorded an interview. every once in a while

32
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,000
You come across people who you're pretty sure you've known for a really long time,

33
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:37,000
even though you've just met them.

34
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Bailey is one such person.

35
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,000
He is the author of The Commissioner's Corner.

36
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
It's a great book about scouting that I want to highly recommend to you,

37
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:52,000
And Darnall was good enough to take the time to sit down with me last week

38
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,000
and record the interview that you're about to hear,

39
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:00,000
And I think it's great listening because Darnall has a very infectious enthusiasm

40
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,000
and cheerfulness that we all could really use.

41
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:08,000
So, without further ado, I'm going to take you right to that interview with Darnall daily,

42
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
and that's going to take up the rest of the podcast.

43
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:12,000
So let's get started, shall we?

44
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:39,000
F Darnall Daily Jr might be a familiar name to you.

45
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:42,000
He has a long record of service to scouting

46
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:45,000
And we're going to find out a little bit more about that.

47
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,000
And he has plenty of unit level experience too,

48
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,000
And we're going to find out a little bit more about that.

49
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,000
But you may know Darnall through his book The Commissioner's Corner,

50
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,000
which is a collection of essays and thoughts and poems

51
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,000
that reflect his love for and appreciation of scouting,

52
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,000
And I'm happy to welcome him to join us on the podcast today

53
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:08,000
from his home in Redding Pennsylvania.

54
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,000
How are you doing, Darnall?

55
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:11,000
I'm doing really well.

56
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:13,000
You started out as a Cub Scout.

57
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,000
I was a Cub Scout. yes,

58
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:21,000
I was in PAC 35 in the Baltimore Air Council, chartered to Church of the Redeemer.

59
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:25,000
That would have been 47,, 48, and 49.

60
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:29,000
I think, if I remember correctly, my mother was involved with the den.

61
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:34,000
So I became 12 and then went into the troop, troop 35,,

62
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,000
still at Church of the Redeemer. instead,

63
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,000
They've had well over 300 Eagle Scouts in this troop.

64
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,000
The troop is still there.

65
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:46,000
My patrol leader was a guy by the name of Charlie Starr,

66
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:49,000
And he was the Eagle Scout number seven in the troop.

67
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,000
And then of course that September went to the military school

68
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:56,000
and that kind of cut my Boy Scout career short.

69
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:01,000
There was a reason. mother sent me to that military boarding school- you know,

70
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:06,000
I was just reading about that- as a reward for your good conduct.

71
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Exactly right, yes,

72
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:14,000
And you have to take my word for that, because mother's long gone. you know,

73
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:24,000
I met with a bunch of those guys for dinner last Saturday night down in Baltimore

74
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:30,000
And one of my classmates was a four-star Navy admiral

75
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:34,000
and always thought that he was the exception to the rule,

76
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:39,000
that his parents had really sent him there for a good education

77
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,000
and not because of behavior problems.

78
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:48,000
And so I invited him up next to Reading to be a speaker at one of our fundraising dinners,

79
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:54,000
And at the dinner he confessed that just before he was sent to the military boarding school

80
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:57,000
he had been on a Boy Scout camp out

81
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:01,000
And he was not the one that set off the firecrackers,

82
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:06,000
but he was the one that brought them to the camp out.

83
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:10,000
And so there are no exceptions.

84
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:16,000
So the four-star admiral, the immediate past president of AARP,

85
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:22,000
past chairman and CEO of Saks Fifth Avenue, and that's just my class.

86
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:27,000
Go from scouts, you go to military school,

87
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:34,000
then we skip forward to you becoming reacquainted with scouting and getting involved again.

88
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:35,000
How did that happen?

89
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:40,000
Well, my son's joined scouting

90
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,000
Started with. my older son was in the cub pack.

91
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:50,000
I was not officially involved then, but when he joined the scout troop,

92
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:56,000
which was a troop of 423 in the Delaney District in Baltimore Area Council,

93
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:00,000
I went to pick him up one night and the scout master says:

94
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:03,000
hey, why don't you hang around and help me out?

95
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:07,000
You know, just one hour a week, you know the usual one.

96
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:10,000
And I said: well, geez, I don't know anything about scouting.

97
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:12,000
You know, I was only in for a few minutes.

98
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:15,000
So he said: well, don't worry, we'll get you training.

99
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:17,000
I said, well, okay, I'll hang around and help you out.

100
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:21,000
So that kid was 11 then and he's 57 now.

101
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
So that was a few years ago.

102
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:25,000
So I became assistant scout master

103
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,000
And then this guy says: you know,

104
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:29,000
I promised that we would get you training,

105
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:32,000
but I realized I'd never taken the training.

106
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:35,000
So he and I took- I think it was- scout master- fundamentals.

107
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:38,000
We went a couple of three nights and then a weekend

108
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,000
And he and I went through that

109
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:43,000
And then he did wood batch that year

110
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:46,000
And then I did wood batch the next year,

111
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:47,000
1972.

112
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,000
And long about that time he said:

113
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:52,000
well, you know, it's your turn to be scout master now.

114
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:53,000
Yeah, okay.

115
00:07:54,000 --> 00:08:03,000
So I was scout master of Troop 423 in Baltimore Council for six or seven years

116
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:09,000
And then I lost my job in Baltimore and had to move to Massachusetts.

117
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:13,000
I became assistant district commissioner in the Sama set district

118
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:18,000
in the old colony council. up there in the old Boy Scout handbook

119
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:22,000
where it showed you the directions for how to put your council strip in,

120
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:26,000
It said: Sama set council did that for about a year

121
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:31,000
And I had two scouting executives come to my office-

122
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:34,000
Never a good sign, right,

123
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:38,000
And we want you to become the district commissioner.

124
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:39,000
Yeah,

125
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:40,000
Okay.

126
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:46,000
So I became the district commissioner and it really was a dysfunctional district.

127
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:50,000
There never was a chairman. in the time I was there

128
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:54,000
I wound up doing the roundtables myself.

129
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:59,000
I even ran a scout master fundamental course myself-

130
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:02,000
I think that for about a year and a half,

131
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:06,000
And realized I hadn't seen a Boy Scout in all that time.

132
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:09,000
And you know, no chairman, no committee meetings,

133
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:13,000
I think three different executives in a year and a half.

134
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:18,000
Anybody who's been involved in scouting has seen those situations.

135
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,000
So I quit

136
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:24,000
And having been the district commissioner for a year and a half,

137
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:29,000
I knew where the functional units were and where the nonfunctional unit went.

138
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:36,000
And one of the units that was really functional was Troop 101 in Marshfield, Massachusetts.

139
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:40,000
I went to that and I said I would like another assistant scout master.

140
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:42,000
Oh yeah, I would like you.

141
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:46,000
So I was an assistant scout master there for the rest of the time

142
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:48,000
and I was in New England

143
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:52,000
And then I lost my job again, moved to Redding Pennsylvania.

144
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:58,000
So I called up the office and talked to the executive and said: hey, I'm in town

145
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:05,000
And I didn't realize that that's a red flag to executives when you call up and volunteer.

146
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,000
Not too many people do that,

147
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
That's right.

148
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:15,000
And he was a new council executive and he said: oh okay,

149
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:16,000
Why don't we meet?

150
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:21,000
So I became an assistant council commissioner for training.

151
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:25,000
I did that for about a year, a year and a half.

152
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:30,000
I did a stint for a year as the vice president relationships.

153
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:34,000
You know I had all the religious relationships and membership committee and that kind of thing.

154
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
I did that for a year.

155
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:42,000
The guy that was the council commissioner kept saying I don't know why I'm doing this.

156
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:44,000
Donald's the one that ought to be doing it.

157
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:47,000
He knows much more about scouting than I do.

158
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:54,000
So after his year-long campaign in May of 1986, I was elected council commissioner

159
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
And I did that for 16 years.

160
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
Wow, That's a long time,

161
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:00,000
Much too long.

162
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:07,000
And it wasn't until I stopped being that that I realized that Roger did this too long.

163
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:11,000
Well, nobody tried to unseat you- apparently,

164
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
Apparently not.

165
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:18,000
And we basically had the same council executive all that time,

166
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,000
A fellow by the name of Dick Bennett.

167
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:23,000
He was council executive in Chester County council.

168
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:24,000
Yeah, that's my council.

169
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:26,000
I remember Dick very fondly

170
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
And what a wonderful guy,

171
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:32,000
And so he and I were good buddies and that worked out well.

172
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:36,000
About 20 minutes after I was elected council commissioner,

173
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:41,000
I got a phone call and said: is your article ready for the newsletter?

174
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:44,000
You know the stuff they don't tell you.

175
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:49,000
And I said: well, no, tell me what you have in mind.

176
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:54,000
And I said: well, the council commissioner always writes a monthly column in the newsletter.

177
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:56,000
And I said: okay, when do you need this?

178
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:59,000
Well, we're hoping to put that to bed by five o'clock tonight,

179
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:03,000
You know. and so that was every month for the first couple of years.

180
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:07,000
In all there were 144 of those little articles.

181
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:10,000
I guess about a hundred of them were in the book.

182
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
And that's your book, The Commissioner School.

183
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
Yes, exactly right.

184
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:17,000
I recommend it to anybody who's listening.

185
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:18,000
It's a great read

186
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:23,000
And there's a lot of good, I would say, old school scouting in it.

187
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:24,000
How about this?

188
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:27,000
How about we call it timeless scouting wisdom?

189
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:28,000
How about that?

190
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:33,000
Well, some of the things in there have definitely stood the test of time.

191
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:36,000
Two things in there that I get requests for all the time.

192
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:40,000
One is my poem The Boy Scout's Mother Asked.

193
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
I read that at Eagle presentations.

194
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:46,000
Still 12 years after I stopped in Council, Commissioner,

195
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:50,000
I still get invited to do Eagle presentations.

196
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:53,000
And then the other one is Guardian of the Gate,

197
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:58,000
which was reprinted two years ago in the advancement newsletter

198
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:05,000
And there's now a video of me on the National Boy Scout website reading that,

199
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:13,000
That particular essay. I think what's so effective about it is there's a picture of a gate that you form in your mind.

200
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:17,000
Tell us a little bit about what you were driving at

201
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:24,000
All of those articles. something happened that led me to want to deliver a message

202
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:32,000
And I kept running into- and I still run into today- people who are saying advancement is the issue.

203
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:38,000
We have to be strict on the boys to make sure that they don't get advancement they don't deserve.

204
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:42,000
And the truth is we want to work the other way.

205
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:49,000
We want to make sure that all of the boys that deserve any kind of advancement get it,

206
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:54,000
Because advancement is just a tool we use to keep kids in scouting so we can influence their character.

207
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
That's what I was getting at,

208
00:13:56,000 --> 00:14:02,000
Just an article written out of frustration with people trying to prevent kids from advancing.

209
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:06,000
Shortly after I had came, Council Commissioner, this woman calls me up and she said:

210
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:12,000
you know, my son turned in his Eagle application two days after his birthday

211
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:17,000
And they're saying he turned it in late and can't get his Eagle.

212
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:20,000
I said let me check into it and I'll get back to you.

213
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:25,000
At that point I was not that familiar with the forms or anything. so I called the office

214
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:29,000
and I get the Director of Field Service and I said: how about lunch?

215
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:32,000
And would you bring with you this kid's papers

216
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:37,000
And also bring with you what we show the kids for their getting ready for Eagle?

217
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:43,000
I meet this guy for lunch and I look at the application and across the top of it, in red letters. it says:

218
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:48,000
all the work has to be done before your 18th birthday, but the form can be turned in later.

219
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:52,000
I said: so why are we turning kids down? because they turned the form in?

220
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:55,000
Well, that's what the Advancement Chairman has said we have to do.

221
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
I said, okay, here's what we're going to do.

222
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:01,000
We're going to get this kid a Board of Review and by the end of the day,

223
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:05,000
I want a new Advancement Chairman for this council. and there was-

224
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:08,000
And I would keep running into this-

225
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:13,000
There are- people that were making kids stay under the tension that a Board of Review

226
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:17,000
and Scoutmasters stopping kids from advancing because they were too young,

227
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:20,000
and all of the nonsense you hear.

228
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:25,000
One day I'm writing my article and I don't know what I'll write, and I wrote the Guardian of the Gate

229
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:29,000
and it's nice image because that's what they were doing.

230
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:40,000
You could just see a Scoutmaster carrying a car, being a total arm, standing in front of a bob wire gate.

231
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:43,000
Yeah, I mean, it's a common frustration.

232
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:46,000
It's something that we run into time and time again.

233
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:52,000
I will tell you that as a younger man, as a Scoutmaster, I encountered that same kind of frustration.

234
00:15:52,000 --> 00:16:00,000
I looked at things and you have this kind of unsettled feeling that some boys are getting away with something

235
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:03,000
And so you decide that's it,

236
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:10,000
We're going to tighten up and you begin making your own rules and doing the things that you just mentioned.

237
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:14,000
Did you experience that as a Scouter at all?

238
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:17,000
No, I really didn't.

239
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,000
We were too busy having fun

240
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:23,000
As a Scoutmaster. we did a lot of campouts.

241
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:26,000
I don't ever remember wanting to clamp down.

242
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:33,000
You know I'm taking wood badge right from the beginning helped me, and we took the leadership development wood badge

243
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:41,000
Years later I found out that that was the experimental course, because that didn't become official until 1972.

244
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:49,000
I think that experience helped me to be more involved with how the kids were developing.

245
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:53,000
I'm not sure what prompted that, but I don't ever remember being frustrated.

246
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:56,000
Well, it took me a while to work my way through that.

247
00:16:56,000 --> 00:17:02,000
For a lot of us there's a level of hardheadedness that you have to have to volunteer to be a Scout leader,

248
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:03,000
Right?

249
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:08,000
But boy, it's just so much more fun and it's so much more rewarding.

250
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:13,000
and I think Scouts get a whole lot more out of the program when you realize that, as you say,

251
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:17,000
you're not trying to guard against them getting something they don't deserve.

252
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:20,000
You're working at making sure they get all they deserve

253
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:21,000
Right,

254
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,000
And you do that because that keeps them in there.

255
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:27,000
There's a hook of being rewarded for your accomplishments

256
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
And I certainly didn't know that then.

257
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:37,000
I mean, I think a positive reinforcement is something I've come to understand, like in the last 15 years-

258
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:40,000
Certainly not 40 years ago,

259
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:44,000
And you would see the kids advance and see them develop.

260
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:51,000
Take a kid out on Friday night and you would bring a different boy back to his mother on Sunday night.

261
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:54,000
They just changed that fast at that age.

262
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:59,000
Anything that kept them in the program long enough for that change to happen was worthwhile,

263
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
Besides which we were having fun.

264
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
It is a blast, isn't it?

265
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:09,000
You can't be a Scout leader and not enjoy those kind of activities.

266
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:16,000
I have my rules for life, and rule number four says no man ever matures past 12.

267
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:22,000
And I've never had a woman disagree with me on that.

268
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:28,000
You know the really best Scout masters or guys that didn't get to eight.

269
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:33,000
The other thing that helped me through that was seeing the effect on my boys,

270
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:39,000
My teenage boys. I got the same nonsense in any parent gets when you said, hey, son.

271
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:44,000
But then if I said Mr Patrol Leader or Mr Senior Patrol Leader.

272
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:49,000
Now, all of a sudden we have a conversation, and that was enlightening.

273
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:58,000
If you could go back to that man who was a newly minted Scout leader, with the perspective of years,

274
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:03,000
I'm sure you've aided a lot of people and counseled a lot of Scouters over time.

275
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,000
But what do you tell that guy?

276
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:08,000
Maybe somebody is a little down and discouraged.

277
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:11,000
Maybe things aren't working quite the way that they think they ought to-

278
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:15,000
And you've got the ear of a number of people just like that right now.

279
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
What do you tell that guy?

280
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,000
I tell them to obey the Scout law.

281
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:23,000
And the most important point of the Scout law is the Scout is cheerful.

282
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:30,000
You know, if we can teach kids anything, it's to enjoy life with a smile.

283
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:35,000
Anything else that happens is just icing on the cake.

284
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:42,000
As an early Scout leader, I think I came to an epiphany realizing that the Scout law

285
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:48,000
was not only something I'm teaching the kids, but something that I ought to be working toward too.

286
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:53,000
I adopted the Scout as cheerful and worked at that, and it's been with me ever since.

287
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:57,000
The only thing I can say that would be different is: I came by it naturally.

288
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:00,000
My mother was a naturally cheerful person.

289
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:05,000
Toward the end of her life. she was paralyzed for the last 11 years of her life

290
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:09,000
and yet she was the life of the party in the nursing home.

291
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:14,000
As much as I'd like to give myself credit, I think it was in my genes.

292
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:18,000
All I had to do was have it awakened,

293
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:22,000
You know, be careful about it, have fun, enjoy what's going on, enjoy your life.

294
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
Yeah, that's kind of contagious, isn't it?

295
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:25,000
Yes, yes,

296
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:28,000
Laugh and the world laughs with you. cry and you cry alone.

297
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:34,000
And the other thing I was able to do is I was able to let the kids run the troop,

298
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:39,000
And I came by that by accident, you know, and I saw how well it worked.

299
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:46,000
I was still the assistant Scoutmaster and one of the kids came to me and he said:

300
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
we want to go on a canoe trip.

301
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:53,000
The Scoutmaster, Mr Levitt, can't go when we want to go on a canoe trip.

302
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:57,000
And he says: if we want to go, you have to go with us.

303
00:20:57,000 --> 00:21:02,000
And I said: well, okay, but you're going to have to organize it because I'm really busy at work.

304
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
You're going to have to take care of it.

305
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:09,000
And this kid says: okay, Mr Daily, he hires canoes and he arranges for transportation

306
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:14,000
and he arranges for the food and he arranges everything,

307
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:19,000
And we drive 100 miles away from home to go on this three-day canoe trip

308
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:21,000
and everything went like clockwork.

309
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,000
Isn't that interesting.

310
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:28,000
I was a little kid and at the time I was a general manager in the business I was in.

311
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:33,000
I could get one of my supervisors at work to be this responsible and this resourceful.

312
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:34,000
Wouldn't that be neat?

313
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
And so that's what I started preaching.

314
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:40,000
I would say: what do you want to do? How do you want to do it? Where do you want to go?

315
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:43,000
So we ran a boy run troop.

316
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:49,000
Coming from that experience, as I played other roles, I relied on just having that kid.

317
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:55,000
He, incidentally, went on to sell insurance and was a million-dollar roundtable guy,

318
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:59,000
Whatever that would they have. many times over.

319
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:02,000
That showed me those two things:

320
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:05,000
What the kids wanted and being cheerful about it.

321
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:10,000
Those two things really helped me, not only in scouting but in life.

322
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:14,000
So while they're important- all the rules and the regulations

323
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:18,000
and being a world-class outdoorsman and stuff like that-

324
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:23,000
is secondary to the attitude to which you approach all of this.

325
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:26,000
Yeah, exactly right, You don't have to be a woodsman.

326
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:32,000
It's nice if you know one where you can say, hey, what do we do now?

327
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:35,000
One of the things that I'm very curious about.

328
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:39,000
I passed the 50 barrier about four years ago,

329
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:40,000
Okay,

330
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:43,000
So you've got a couple of decades on me.

331
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:49,000
I see that age and experience in scouting seems to be a sword that can cut both ways.

332
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:53,000
I've seen guys who've been around for a few decades

333
00:22:53,000 --> 00:23:00,000
and they seem to become kind of grumbly, cane-waving old complainers,

334
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:01,000
Yeah.

335
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:03,000
And then I meet guys like you.

336
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:07,000
Like you said, you really never grew past being 12 years old.

337
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:09,000
No, no, absolutely not.

338
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:13,000
And so is there a secret there?

339
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:19,000
Is there anything that a guy in my position can do

340
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:23,000
so that he doesn't end up being one of those cane-waving old complainee guys?

341
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:27,000
Yeah, the scout won't.

342
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:29,000
The scout is careful.

343
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:32,000
Oh, come on, That's too hard,

344
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:33,000
That's too hard.

345
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:36,000
There's not a pill you can take or anything like that.

346
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:38,000
I don't think so. no,

347
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:41,000
Although God knows, my doctor has me taking enough pills.

348
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:52,000
What I think I get from your book and from your experiences is that this is all really pretty simple.

349
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:57,000
There's just a couple of things to really focus on and the rest of it seems to follow.

350
00:23:57,000 --> 00:23:59,000
I think that's true.

351
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:02,000
I used to tell scouts and I'd tell myself:

352
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:06,000
you get up every morning and you have a choice:

353
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:09,000
You could enjoy life or you could not.

354
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:15,000
I went through a period of my life, about 54 months, where I had eight different jobs,

355
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:22,000
and years later I came across this three by five card that I carried in my pocket during that time,

356
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:27,000
And on one side it was not my fault. and you turn it over on the other side

357
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:31,000
and it says: and I am nine foot tall and I can walk through walls,

358
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:35,000
And I remember standing in front of the mirror in the morning reciting that

359
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:38,000
so I would go forth with a cheerful attitude,

360
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:41,000
And I think that's what you have to do.

361
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:47,000
So being cheerful isn't something that you're walking along one day and it just jumps on your back.

362
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:49,000
No, you have to work at it.

363
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:54,000
You do actually have to work at it, because every morning it doesn't roll around and you feel fantastic.

364
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:56,000
Well, exactly right,

365
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:58,000
And that's a hard thing to teach.

366
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:01,000
I used to tell the kids exactly that.

367
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:05,000
Why did you decide this morning to be whoopee?

368
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:07,000
Well, I don't know, Mr Taylor.

369
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:09,000
Well, okay,

370
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:12,000
I really do appreciate you spending the time with us. Darnell,

371
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:17,000
You're an inspiration to me personally and, I'm sure, to many, many other scouts,

372
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:22,000
and we look forward to hearing from you again.

373
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:26,000
I appreciate you calling and talking to you

374
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:31,000
and I appreciate what you and thousands of other scouts do

375
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:35,000
to try to make the world better for the young boys.

376
00:25:57,000 --> 00:26:02,000
Well, thanks for listening to this 222nd edition of the Scoutmaster Podcast.

